docs/decisions.md:1153:| OD38 | <a id="od38"></a>The root resource API (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD8). Options: `fn main(root: Root) -> u8` with trusted splitting functions (`root.split_alloc()`, `root.split_console()`, ...) returning linear tokens; or a fixed set of token parameters `main` may declare (`fn main(alloc: Alloc, io: Console)`). Recommendation: the single `Root` value, as Austral's `RootCapability`, split by trusted functions the wrapper gate lists Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): `fn main(root: Root) -> u8` (or Unit), `Root` split into linear tokens by trusted functions; specify the split functions and how leftover authority is consumed | `main` takes no parameters today (STMT-7) | R-0557 (TRUST-9) and STMT-7 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Superseded by the authority decision of 2026-11-01: there is no `Root` type; `main`'s parameters are the target's singletons, hosted `fn main() with(...) -> u8` and freestanding `fn main(uart: Uart, arena: Arena) with(Device, Alloc) -> u8`; revised 2026-11-02: `main` receives the target's grants and returns `u8` only ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
docs/decisions.md:1154:| OD39 | <a id="od39"></a>The interface artifact format (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD9). Options: a generated `.coni` text file per module beside the build output; a JSON interface in the build directory; or the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source. Recommendation: rendered Concrete source (declarations without bodies), deterministic and reviewable as a diff Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source without bodies, generated by the compiler, with an API-diff gate; it also carries private layout, impl methods, attributes, constants, capabilities and generic bodies, and is not read as FFI-6 bodyless foreign bindings | the compiler has the declaration-level `FileSummary` and `--report interface`, but no persisted per-module interface artifact and no API-diff gate of the proposed form | R-0556 (MOD-7) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `pub` decls rendered as Concrete source, compiler-generated, API-diff gate |
docs/decisions.md:1160:| OD45 | <a id="od45"></a>Capability-variable inference (added 2026-10-29, from bug 063's ruling). Main today binds a callee's `cap C` variable from the capability set of an argument's function type when its type peek (`PeekType`) can compute that type without checking the argument, and refuses a variable no argument determines (E0242, `pick_generic::<i64>()`). Options: (a) specify the current compiler's peek-based rule, listing the argument forms it covers; (b) bind from the checked type of every argument, whatever its form; (c) require an explicit capability argument whenever an argument is not a name. Recommendation: (b), so whether a call infers does not depend on the argument's spelling Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (b) bind from the checked type of every argument; refuse unresolved or inconsistent ones; no result-context inference | the current compiler is (a) without the list | TRUST-4, TYPE-1; bug 063's record | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Bind from checked type of every argument; no result-context. **Revised by the owner 2026-11-05 (row Q5.1/Q5.4, [revision](#decided-2026-11-05-revisions)): capability arguments are written explicitly at every polymorphic call and checked; no capability inference** |
docs/decisions.md:1161:| OD46 | <a id="od46"></a>Generic type-argument inference (added 2026-10-29, from bug 308's ruling). Main today binds each type parameter from the types its peek (`PeekType`) computes for the arguments given for it, a value `if` or `match` included since bug 308, but answers `.placeholder` for a generic call's result and some arms with binders, and refuses a parameter no argument determines; it never infers from the expected result type. Options: (a) specify the current compiler's peek-based rule, listing the forms it covers; (b) bind from the checked type of every argument, whatever its form; (c) also infer from the expected type at the call site. Recommendation: (b), local and predictable, with explicit type arguments for the rest; Austral infers type parameters from the arguments, and a `let` declaration's type can disambiguate a return-type-polymorphic call Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (b) from checked argument types only, never from the expected result; arguments that need an expected type are refused, not defaulted | the current compiler is (a) without the list | TYPE-13, TYPE-15; bug 308's record | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): From checked argument types only |
docs/decisions.md:1221:| OD106 | <a id="od106"></a>The result type of a checked conversion (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a checked conversion (`try_convert`) reports a value it cannot preserve as `Option::None` or as a `Result` error carrying the reason. Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10. The current compiler: no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 wrote `try_convert::<T>(x) -> Option<T>` "(or a `Result` with a reason)" and left the choice to the API's owner. Options: (a) `Option<T>`; (b) a `Result` whose error names the reason (out of range, not integral, NaN, infinite, not representable). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500) | ARITH-5, ARITH-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `Result<T, OutOfRange>` (failure, not absence) |
docs/decisions.md:1228:| OD113 | <a id="od113"></a>The ownership exceptions of trusted code (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether trusted code keeps its two exceptions to the ownership rules, destroying a value through a reference and releasing with `vec_free` a vector whose elements are not Copy. Bears on TRUST-5, TRUST-12, OWN-5, OWN-8. The current compiler: both are accepted in trusted code only (elsewhere E0290 under OWN-8 and E0287 under OWN-5): std's collections destroy an element through `(p as &T)` in drop glue after its owner was consumed, and a trusted function may free a buffer it has emptied. The archived [MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md) §11 said that `trusted` does not relax linearity, and TRUST-5's rationale says the same. Options: (a) keep both, each a named trusted operation with its premise (the owner was consumed; the vector is empty); (b) remove both, drop glue moving the element out of raw memory instead (TRUST-7); (c) keep only destruction through a reference; (d) keep only `vec_free` of an emptied vector. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | both accepted in trusted code only (E0290, E0287 elsewhere) | TRUST-5, TRUST-12, OWN-5, OWN-8 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove both |
docs/decisions.md:1232:| OD117 | <a id="od117"></a>`old`, quantifiers and implication in contracts (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether contract expressions gain `old(x)`, quantifiers and an implication or equivalence operator, and with what syntax. Bears on CON-2, CON-3, SYN-1. The current compiler: `#[ensures(result == old(x))]` compiles and runs, and `--report contracts` marks the clause `invalid_contract_expression` (unknown function `old`); `forall i: Int, ...` is a parse error (E0001); there is no implication operator. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Contract Expression V1) lists all three as deferred. Options: (a) add each, with a syntax decided per form; (b) keep them out, a claim needing one being a Lean theorem; (c) refuse `old(..)` now as an unknown name, deciding the rest later. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-117 | see OPEN-117 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No `old`, quantifiers or implication in contracts; state such properties as Lean theorems |
docs/decisions.md:1240:| OD125 | <a id="od125"></a>Contracts at run time (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[requires]`, `#[ensures]` or loop `#[invariant]` that no proof discharges is checked at run time, trapping when it fails, or is only a claim. Bears on CON-1, CON-2, STMT-2. The current compiler: contracts are erased metadata: a call that violates `#[requires(x > 0)]` runs normally, in the binary and in `--interp`, and `assert` elaborates to proof evidence only (syntax chapter introduction). [ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md) lists contract-violation traps among the fatal failures. Options: (a) claims only, never checked at run time, as in the current compiler; (b) checked at run time and trapping unless discharged; (c) checked under a build profile only (OPEN-97, OPEN-124). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-125 | see OPEN-125 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Claims only **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a claim used for safety admission needs closed evidence.** |
docs/decisions.md:1251:| OD136 | <a id="od136"></a>std's `process_exit` (added 2026-10-30, raised while reviewing the absorption of docs/compiler and docs/platform). Question: Whether std keeps `process_exit`, which ends the process through libc `exit` without running any deferred call or consuming the caller's owned values. Bears on STMT-7, OWN-2 and TRUST-4. The current compiler: `std.process.process_exit(code: i32) with(Process)` exists and calls libc `exit`; the language itself has no exit operation (STMT-7). The design record [MAIN_EXIT_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/MAIN_EXIT_MODEL.md) calls an exit operation deliberately absent, since in a linear language it would bypass the proof that every owned value is consumed. Options: (a) keep it, stating that it skips cleanup and that `Process` is what grants it; (b) remove it, status flowing back through `main`'s return only. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the review of the absorption of docs/platform, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-136 | see OPEN-136 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
docs/decisions.md:1264:| OD149 | <a id="od149"></a>How `[e; N]` evaluates `e` (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: How many times an array repetition `[e; N]` evaluates `e`, and which `e` it accepts. Bears on STMT-4, OWN-15 and TYPE-7. The current compiler: `e` is evaluated once per element, in index order (`[bump(&mut n); 3]` gives `[1, 2, 3]` on both consumers), and whether a linear `e` is accepted was not shown (an array of a linear type that is never consumed is refused with E0208 either way). OWN-15 says only that an array literal moves each element in, and STMT-4 orders written elements. Options: (a) `e` is evaluated once and copied, so it must be Copy; (b) `e` is evaluated `N` times in index order, as the current compiler does; (c) `e` must be a constant expression. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-149 | see OPEN-149 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Evaluated once, must be Copy |
