20:**CON-1@1.** A contract claim states partial correctness, holding for every execution that terminates, and a function is claimed to terminate only when `decreasing` evidence for its loops and recursion is checked.
35:### CON-2. What `requires`, `ensures`, `invariant` and `variant` claim
37:**CON-2@1.** `#[requires(p)]` claims that `p` holds at every call (an obligation of each caller, assumed on entry), `#[ensures(q)]` claims that `q` holds at every return with `result` naming the returned value, and a loop's `#[invariant(i)]` and `#[variant(v)]` claim that `i` holds before the loop and after every iteration and that `v` is non-negative and decreases on every iteration.
56:#[ensures(result == n)]
87:**CON-3@2.** Contract expressions are pure non-consuming specifications with mathematical integer arithmetic unless a machine operation is named; function clauses see parameters, permitted declarations and `result` only in `ensures`, postconditions read surviving parameter state after deferred cleanup without an implicit entry snapshot, and loop clauses and assertions use their own lexical scope including permitted ghost locals.
98:**Rationale.** OD143 and OD144 restrict function contracts to their specification scope and post-cleanup surviving state; the audit distinguishes loop and assertion lexical scope. No implicit old snapshot is introduced (OD117).
336:**CON-6@1.** `assert e` claims that `e` holds where it is written, an obligation of the function, and `assume e` makes `e` a hypothesis of the obligations after it whose own evidence is `assumed`, never `proved`, and neither statement executes.
381:**CON-7@1.** `result` names the returned value in an `#[ensures]` clause and is bound in no other contract clause, so a `requires`, `invariant` or `variant` that names it names an unknown variable.
